{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FZdJWM9AiwtEZoeKKzVuTJ","type":"tool_result","content":"---\n# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log\n\nTicket: abcd-2 | Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) | Status: complete\n\nThis PRD is brownfield on top of the abcd-1 foundation (Next.js 14 App\nRouter, React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9, PostgreSQL 16 via Prisma, Zod,\nReact Hook Form, Vitest + Playwright — see `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`).\nNFR-7 forbids re-scaffolding or duplicating what already exists, so these\nADRs only cover the *new* tech decisions this PRD (auth) introduces, plus\nconfirming that everything else is reused unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js (NextAuth v5) Credentials provider, JWT session strategy\n\n**Context:** FR-2, FR-3, FR-7, FR-9, FR-10, FR-11, NFR-1 (CSRF).\n\n**Decision:** Use Auth.js (NextAuth v5) with a single Credentials provider\ncalling `authorize()`, JWT session strategy (8-hour lifetime, refreshed on\nactivity). No OAuth providers, no database session adapter — the PRD is\ncredentials-only and a DB session store would be an unused abstraction for\na single-provider app.\n\n**Consequences:** CSRF protection on all auth forms comes free from Auth.js\n(NFR-1) instead of being hand-rolled. `middleware.ts` (FR-11) and the `jwt`/\n`session` callbacks (FR-9, FR-10) are the integration points later PRDs must\nroute through, not a new auth layer.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: bcrypt (cost 12) with a dummy-compare for timing safety\n\n**Context:** FR-3, FR-5, NFR-2, NFR-3.\n\n**Decision:** `bcrypt` npm package, cost factor 12, for password hashing and\nverification. When the looked-up user doesn't exist, `authorize()` still\nruns a bcrypt compare against a fixed dummy hash before returning failure,\nso unknown-user and wrong-password paths take comparable time (NFR-2).\nCost 12 is kept as-is per NFR-3's p95 < 1s budget rather than tuned down —\nif real hardware ever misses that budget, the cost factor is the one knob\nto revisit.\n\n**Consequences:** `bcrypt` is a new dependency (not present in abcd-1); no\nother hashing library is introduced alongside it.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Node's built-in `crypto` for token generation and hashing\n\n**Context:** FR-1, FR-12, FR-18, NFR-1.\n\n**Decision:** Invite/reset tokens are `crypto.randomBytes(32)` base64url-\nencoded; stored as `crypto.createHash('sha256')` hashes only, never raw.\nLookup compares hashes with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`. No token/crypto\nlibrary is added — Node stdlib covers random generation, hashing, and\nconstant-time comparison outright.\n\n**Consequences:** The raw token exists only in the generated link\n(logged via `sendAuthEmail`, FR-27) and is never persisted or logged\nelsewhere, satisfying NFR-1's \"raw tokens never stored\" rule.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter, no new store\n\n**Context:** FR-6, FR-17.\n\n**Decision:** `src/server/rate-limit.ts` implements the sign-in (10/IP/5min)\nand forgot-password (3/email/hr, 10/IP/hr) limits with an in-memory\nMap-based sliding window, exported for reuse across both call sites. No\nRedis or other external store is introduced for this PRD.\n\n**Consequences:** Limits reset on process restart and don't share state\nacross horizontally-scaled instances.\n`ponytail: in-memory single-instance rate limiter; move to Redis/shared store if the app scales past one instance.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Extend the existing Prisma schema; no new ORM/DB","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc5961f0-9fcd-4cfc-98eb-4498256dcf5a","uuid":"c9db97b6-0ddd-46f6-a308-8e7bb56dce14","timestamp":"2026-10-16T16:40:03.054Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\n# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log\n\nTicket: abcd-2 | Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) | Status: complete\n\nThis PRD is brownfield on top of the abcd-1 foundation (Next.js 14 App\nRouter, React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9, PostgreSQL 16 via Prisma, Zod,\nReact Hook Form, Vitest + Playwright — see `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`).\nNFR-7 forbids re-scaffolding or duplicating what already exists, so these\nADRs only cover the *new* tech decisions this PRD (auth) introduces, plus\nconfirming that everything else is reused unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js (NextAuth v5) Credentials provider, JWT session strategy\n\n**Context:** FR-2, FR-3, FR-7, FR-9, FR-10, FR-11, NFR-1 (CSRF).\n\n**Decision:** Use Auth.js (NextAuth v5) with a single Credentials provider\ncalling `authorize()`, JWT session strategy (8-hour lifetime, refreshed on\nactivity). No OAuth providers, no database session adapter — the PRD is\ncredentials-only and a DB session store would be an unused abstraction for\na single-provider app.\n\n**Consequences:** CSRF protection on all auth forms comes free from Auth.js\n(NFR-1) instead of being hand-rolled. `middleware.ts` (FR-11) and the `jwt`/\n`session` callbacks (FR-9, FR-10) are the integration points later PRDs must\nroute through, not a new auth layer.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: bcrypt (cost 12) with a dummy-compare for timing safety\n\n**Context:** FR-3, FR-5, NFR-2, NFR-3.\n\n**Decision:** `bcrypt` npm package, cost factor 12, for password hashing and\nverification. When the looked-up user doesn't exist, `authorize()` still\nruns a bcrypt compare against a fixed dummy hash before returning failure,\nso unknown-user and wrong-password paths take comparable time (NFR-2).\nCost 12 is kept as-is per NFR-3's p95 < 1s budget rather than tuned down —\nif real hardware ever misses that budget, the cost factor is the one knob\nto revisit.\n\n**Consequences:** `bcrypt` is a new dependency (not present in abcd-1); no\nother hashing library is introduced alongside it.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Node's built-in `crypto` for token generation and hashing\n\n**Context:** FR-1, FR-12, FR-18, NFR-1.\n\n**Decision:** Invite/reset tokens are `crypto.randomBytes(32)` base64url-\nencoded; stored as `crypto.createHash('sha256')` hashes only, never raw.\nLookup compares hashes with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`. No token/crypto\nlibrary is added — Node stdlib covers random generation, hashing, and\nconstant-time comparison outright.\n\n**Consequences:** The raw token exists only in the generated link\n(logged via `sendAuthEmail`, FR-27) and is never persisted or logged\nelsewhere, satisfying NFR-1's \"raw tokens never stored\" rule.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter, no new store\n\n**Context:** FR-6, FR-17.\n\n**Decision:** `src/server/rate-limit.ts` implements the sign-in (10/IP/5min)\nand forgot-password (3/email/hr, 10/IP/hr) limits with an in-memory\nMap-based sliding window, exported for reuse across both call sites. No\nRedis or other external store is introduced for this PRD.\n\n**Consequences:** Limits reset on process restart and don't share state\nacross horizontally-scaled instances.\n`ponytail: in-memory single-instance rate limiter; move to Redis/shared store if the app scales past one instance.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Extend the existing Prisma schema; no new ORM/DB","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
