{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XoEv7h4qrwHVoLr4gdga6F","type":"tool_result","content":"src/config/navigation.ts:4:export type Role = 'ADMIN' | 'TEACHER' | 'STUDENT' | 'PARENT';\nsrc/config/navigation.ts:13:const ALL_ROLES: Role[] = ['ADMIN', 'TEACHER', 'STUDENT', 'PARENT'];\nsrc/config/navigation.ts:20:  { label: 'Dashboard', href: '/dashboard', icon: DashboardOutlinedIcon, roles: ALL_ROLES },\n---\n# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email\n\nTicket: abcd-04 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n\nBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\nNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\nMUI v9 + emotion. This PRD is brownfield on top of that stack — the\ndecisions below are only the deltas this PRD's requirements force, not a\nre-litigation of the base stack.\n\n## ADR-1: Email rendering — `@react-email/components` (new dependency)\n\n**Context.** FR-7/FR-8 require branded HTML+plain-text email templates\n(invite, password-reset, generic) sharing a base layout. The codebase has\nno email-rendering layer today (`sendInvite()` in\n`src/server/services/users.ts` is a logging-only stub per FR-9). NFR-1\nscopes MUI to *visible app UI*; it explicitly exempts email templates\nbecause MUI's `sx`/emotion runtime CSS does not survive email-client HTML\nsanitizers (no `<style>` tag support, inconsistent CSS-in-JS rendering in\nOutlook/Gmail clients).\n\n**Decision.** Add `@react-email/components` (+ `@react-email/render`) as\nthe email templating layer, rendered server-side to both an HTML string and\na plain-text string. This is a new dependency, but it is the one intake\nalready named in `detectionNotes` (\"adds Nodemailer + @react-email/components\nfor the email layer\"), and it's the standard choice for React-authored,\nemail-client-safe markup (table-based layout, inlined styles) — hand-rolling\nthat is a bigger lift than reusing the library intake already flagged.\nTemplates live under `src/server/email/templates/`, outside the app's React\ntree, so they never import MUI or app-side providers.\n\n**Consequences.** One new runtime dependency. Templates are a separate\ncomponent universe from the app (plain React components, no MUI, no\n`'use client'`) — contributors must know not to reach for app UI components\nhere. Base layout (logo/name header, footer with school address + manage-\npreferences link + disclosure sentence) is a single shared component per\nFR-7, so the three templates (FR-8) stay thin.\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, NFR-1, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-2: Email transport — Nodemailer with a console-transport dev fallback\n\n**Context.** FR-10/FR-13 require sending queued emails via SMTP, with env\nvars `SMTP_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS/SECURE`, `EMAIL_FROM`, and an\n`EMAIL_TRANSPORT` switch (`smtp|console`, default `console` in dev) so local\ndev never needs real SMTP creds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `nodemailer` as the SMTP client — it's the de facto\nstandard Node SMTP library, zero-config for any provider the school's ops\nteam points `SMTP_HOST` at, and needs no additional service/queue\ninfrastructure. Wrap it behind a one-function `sendMail()` in\n`src/server/email/transport.ts` that switches on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`: `smtp`\nconstructs a Nodemailer transporter from the env vars, `console` just logs\nsubject/recipient/text (FR-13) and returns success. No new dependency\nbeyond Nodemailer itself — no mail-queue service, no third-party ESP SDK,\nbecause the queue is already modeled in Postgres (`EmailOutbox`, ADR-3).\n\n**Consequences.** Local/dev/CI runs need zero external services. Production\nrequires real SMTP credentials in env; if a future PRD needs provider-specific\nfeatures (templates-as-a-service, bounce webhooks), that's a new ADR, not a","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b09f90f4-bdfe-409b-ac12-21f6b1ffd7b1","uuid":"61f0d1a7-6001-499e-ac1d-d03c9a3bc99b","timestamp":"2